nificant influence over data governance in Brazil. This formal linkage establishes a clear mechanism through which executive policy and resource decisions feed into CNPD deliberations, ensuring continuity of activities and alignment with national policy objectives. The linkage is evidenced by the CNPD‑Executive relationship description and the explicit seat allocation described in CNPD’s governance model [Data: Entities (409); Relationships (389)].\"} ,{\"summary\":\"Broad sectoral participation across judiciary, Prosecution, and internet governance\",\"explanation\":\"CNPD’s multistakeholder design includes the Judiciary, the National Council of Justice, the Public Ministry, CGI.BR, academia, civil society, the productive sector, and labor groups, ensuring diverse perspectives in data governance. The relationships show formal participation of JUDICIÁRIO and CONSELHO NACIONAL DO MINISTÉRIO PÚBLICO in CNPD governance, along with CGI.BR’s representation; this broad participation supports policy legitimacy and balanced governance, while enabling coordination on fundamental rights and enforcement actions. This broad spectrum is documented through multiple CNPD interaction records and sectoral listings within CNPD’s governance framework [Data: Entities (408, 411, 412, 413, 396); Relationships (383, 384, 394, 395, 396)].\"} ,{\"summary\":\"Public‑private flow and societal input through governance bridges\",\"explanation\":\"A cornerstone of CNPD’s design is the flow of societal voices to CNPD and onward to the ANPD, supported by formal bridging mechanisms described as the ‘Ponte entre a Sociedade e o Estado.’ This reflects formal channels for public participation and policy development, amplifying civil‑societal input in governance and enforcement actions. The bridging concept and its role in regulatory actions are explicitly documented, illustrating how CNPD aligns societal input with state action and regulatory implementation [Data: Entities (494); Relationships (478)].